# Netwise -- canonical contributor identities.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   `git shortlog -sn` listed eight authors for a four-person team. Some of
#   that is one person committing from two machines with different
#   `user.name` settings, which is harmless until the moment it is not: this
#   is a capstone marked partly on individual contribution, and `git blame`
#   is the record of who wrote what.
#
#   A .mailmap fixes log, shortlog and blame attribution WITHOUT rewriting
#   history. Nothing about the commits changes; only how git reports them.
#
# HOW TO ADD YOURSELF
#   Format:  Canonical Name <canonical@email>  Other Name <other@email>
#   Then check it worked:  git shortlog -sn
#
# THE RULE APPLIED HERE
#   An identity is mapped only when the EMAIL proves it is the same person.
#   Mapping on a name alone, or on a guess about who someone is, would put a
#   claim about authorship into the repository that nobody verified -- and
#   authorship is exactly the thing this file exists to get right.

Arsh Vhora <vhoraarsh87@gmail.com>          ARSH871-bot <Vhoraarsh87@gmail.com>
Arsh Vhora <vhoraarsh87@gmail.com>          Arsh. <vhoraarsh87@gmail.com>

Ankeet Patel <patelankeet2@gmail.com>       patelankeet2 <patelankeet2@gmail.com>

# --- MAPPED, on the record: NovaFlow <novaflowbuild@gmail.com> -------------
#
#   Three commits on 3 August, including the ENTIRE initial web layer --
#   web/main.py, web/static/app.js, style.css, index.html, mock_findings.py,
#   roughly 1,200 lines, committed as "US-17: dashboard rendering F-1
#   findings, with secure upload".
#
#   Those are precisely Samika's files, US-17 is Samika's story, and
#   CLAUDE.md attributes the dashboard and secure upload to him. The obvious
#   explanation is a second machine with `git config user.email` unset or
#   set to a personal address, which is extremely common.
#
#   But the email matches nobody on the team, so mapping it would be a guess
#   about who wrote a person's largest single contribution. That is the one
#   guess this file must not make.
#
#   CONFIRMED by @SamikaPerera on #127, 18 August -- twice, the second time
#   with the address in backticks because GitHub swallowed the first as an
#   HTML tag. He states it was the global git identity on his machine before
#   he set a repo-local override partway through the project, and that those
#   three commits are his.
#
#   So the rule at the top of this file is satisfied. The identity is mapped
#   because the person said so where anyone can check it, not because the
#   files looked like his. They did look like his -- that was never the
#   difficulty, and it is exactly why the guess had to be refused until he
#   answered.

Samika Perera <samika.ashen@gmail.com>      NovaFlow <novaflowbuild@gmail.com>

# --- A CORRECTION, because this file is about getting numbers right --------
#
#   Commit 2b824cd's message says "Samika 30 commits, NovaFlow absent".
#   @patelankeet2 requested changes on #168 saying he measured 27, twice.
#   He is right that 30 was wrong, and recounting made it worse than a
#   miscount. Measured across every ref it could plausibly have meant:
#
#       ref                          ashen   nova   total
#       2b824cd itself                  24      3      27
#       origin/main at that moment      25      3      28
#       the branch head today           24      3      27
#       origin/main today               27      3      30
#
#   **30 was true of nothing at the time it was written.** It has since
#   become true of `main` by drift -- so anyone re-checking it today, on
#   main, would confirm a claim that was false when made. A wrong number
#   that later becomes right by accident is worse than one that stays
#   wrong, because the obvious way to check it agrees with it.
#
#   The lesson is not "recount more carefully". It is that a commit count
#   is meaningless without a ref and a date, and this file's own subject is
#   attribution claims nobody verified. So:
#
#       As of 22 August 2026, on origin/main: Samika Perera has 28 commits
#       under samika.ashen@gmail.com and 3 under novaflowbuild@gmail.com.
#
#   Both numbers move. Re-measure rather than quoting these.
#
#   THIRD ROUND, and it is about METHOD rather than arithmetic.
#   @patelankeet2 re-checked and measured 28/3/31 where the line above said
#   27/3/30. He is right about today. He also concluded it was "already 28"
#   when I wrote 27, and that part does not follow from the command he used:
#
#       git log origin/main --until=<T> ...        -> 28 at EVERY cutoff tried
#       git rev-list -1 --before=<T> origin/main   -> a main where it was 27
#
#   `--until` filters TODAY'S main by committer date. It counts a commit
#   authored before T but MERGED after it, so it cannot answer "what did
#   main contain at T" -- which is the question. `rev-list --before` picks
#   the tip as it actually stood. Both numbers are correct answers to
#   different questions, and the disagreement was the two questions, not
#   the two people.
#
#   So 27 was right for main when written, and 28 is right for main now:
#   origin/main has moved 47 commits ahead of this branch since. Which is
#   the same conclusion as the paragraph above, arrived at a second time,
#   by a second person, using a command that quietly answers a different
#   question. Recorded because a measurement that LOOKS like it settles a
#   dispute and does not is exactly this project's recurring shape.
#
#   SECOND CORRECTION, and the more serious one. "NovaFlow absent" was not
#   a measurement at all -- it described the state AFTER this pull request
#   merges. On `main` today NovaFlow is still a distinct author with three
#   commits, precisely because the line above has not landed yet. Writing
#   the post-merge state in the past tense is the same defect as the count,
#   and in a file whose entire purpose is not making confident claims about
#   authorship that nobody checked.
